1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:11,577
At about twenty-five past seven in the evening, on the sixth of May, 1937, the largest aircraft ever built was coming in to land at Lakehurst, New Jersey.

2
00:00:12,877 --> 00:00:22,663
Mooring lines had been dropped to the ground crew below. Reporters and newsreel cameras were waiting. Families of passengers were watching from the edge of the field.

3
00:00:23,963 --> 00:00:30,806
Then a small flame appeared near the tail. In the next half minute or so, the whole ship was consumed by fire.

4
00:00:32,106 --> 00:00:38,399
It fell to the ground, burning, nose up, as people inside jumped or were thrown from its windows.

5
00:00:39,699 --> 00:00:50,425
Thirty-six people died. Remarkably, sixty-two of the ninety-seven people on board survived. But the age of the passenger airship died with the Hindenburg that evening.

6
00:00:52,825 --> 00:01:07,285
The Hindenburg was a German airship, a rigid zeppelin, built by the Zeppelin company and launched in 1936. It was two hundred and forty-five metres long, more than three times the length of a modern jumbo jet.

7
00:01:08,585 --> 00:01:22,614
Inside its aluminium frame were sixteen enormous gas bags, holding about seven million cubic feet of hydrogen. That was what lifted it. Hydrogen is the lightest gas there is. It is also extremely flammable.

8
00:01:23,914 --> 00:01:37,926
The ship had originally been designed to use helium, which is almost as light, and does not burn. But almost all of the world's helium came from the United States, and American law restricted its export. Germany could not get enough.

9
00:01:39,226 --> 00:01:51,608
By the 1930s, the Zeppelin company had been taken under the influence of Hitler's government, and the Hindenburg carried the swastika on its tail fins. It was a flying piece of national prestige.

10
00:01:52,908 --> 00:02:05,266
On board, it was a luxury liner. There were cabins, a dining room, a lounge, promenade decks with sloping windows, and even a pressurised smoking room, sealed off from the hydrogen above.

11
00:02:06,566 --> 00:02:20,132
In its first season, 1936, it made seventeen round trips across the Atlantic without serious incident, to North America and to Brazil. Crossing took about two to three days, much faster than an ocean liner.

12
00:02:22,532 --> 00:02:33,845
On the third of May, 1937, the Hindenburg left Frankfurt on its first North American trip of the new season. It carried thirty-six passengers and sixty-one crew.

13
00:02:35,145 --> 00:02:44,520
It was commanded by Captain Max Pruss. Also on board was Ernst Lehmann, one of Germany's most experienced airship captains, travelling as an observer.

14
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:58,694
Strong headwinds slowed the crossing. When the ship reached the American coast on the afternoon of the sixth of May, it was already behind schedule. It flew over Manhattan, where crowds came out to watch.

15
00:02:59,994 --> 00:03:10,117
Then the weather turned. Thunderstorms were passing over the naval air station at Lakehurst. The landing was delayed while the Hindenburg waited off the coast for them to clear.

16
00:03:11,417 --> 00:03:20,095
By early evening, the storm had moved through, but the air was still damp and electrically charged. The ship came in to land, with orders to hurry.

17
00:03:22,495 --> 00:03:33,344
On the final approach, the Hindenburg made a sharp turn to line up with the mooring mast. To trim the ship, the crew released water ballast from the front, and valved off hydrogen from the back.

18
00:03:34,644 --> 00:03:43,257
At around seven twenty-one, the mooring lines were dropped from the nose. Ground crew grabbed them. The ship hung about two hundred feet in the air.

19
00:03:44,557 --> 00:03:55,232
A few minutes later, some witnesses saw something like a ripple in the fabric near the top of the tail, or a faint blue glow. Then a burst of flame appeared just ahead of the upper fin.

20
00:03:56,532 --> 00:03:58,622
It was about twenty-five past seven.

21
00:03:59,922 --> 00:04:08,303
The fire spread forward through the hydrogen cells with terrifying speed. The tail, losing lift, dropped. The nose pointed up.

22
00:04:09,603 --> 00:04:17,150
In roughly thirty-two to thirty-seven seconds, depending on who measured it, the ship went from first flame to a burning wreck on the ground.

23
00:04:19,550 --> 00:04:34,357
Inside, people had only seconds to react. Some passengers jumped from the windows of the promenade deck as the ship came down. Those who jumped from low enough often survived. Those who jumped too early, from too high, did not.

24
00:04:35,657 --> 00:04:56,280
One family was in the dining room. As the ship neared the ground, the mother threw her two sons out of a window, and then jumped herself. All three survived, though the boys' father and sister did not. The younger boy, eight-year-old Werner Doehner, was the last living survivor of the Hindenburg until he died in 2019.

25
00:04:57,580 --> 00:05:06,188
On the ground, members of the naval ground crew ran towards the burning wreck to pull people out. One of them, Allen Hagaman, was killed.

26
00:05:07,488 --> 00:05:16,845
Thirteen passengers and twenty-two crew members died, along with Hagaman. Ernst Lehmann was carried out alive, badly burned. He died the next day.

27
00:05:19,245 --> 00:05:27,476
Among the reporters at Lakehurst was Herbert Morrison, from a Chicago radio station, recording a report on disc to be broadcast the next day.

28
00:05:28,776 --> 00:05:39,335
His voice, breaking as he watched the ship burn, became one of the most famous recordings ever made. It's burst into flames, he cried, and then, oh, the humanity.

29
00:05:40,635 --> 00:05:52,801
Newsreel cameras filmed the whole thing. Within days, audiences in cinemas around the world watched the Hindenburg burn. It was one of the first great disasters that the public could see with their own eyes.

30
00:05:55,201 --> 00:06:07,774
American and German investigators reached broadly the same conclusion. Hydrogen had been leaking somewhere near the tail, perhaps from a gas cell damaged during the sharp turn. And something had ignited it.

31
00:06:09,074 --> 00:06:23,544
The most likely spark was static electricity. The ship had flown through charged, stormy air. When its wet mooring lines touched the ground, the frame was earthed, but parts of the outer skin may not have been. A discharge could have jumped across.

32
00:06:24,844 --> 00:06:39,065
Theories of sabotage, popular at the time, were never supported by evidence. Later researchers have argued about how much the ship's coated fabric skin helped the fire spread. But without the hydrogen, there would have been no disaster.

33
00:06:41,465 --> 00:06:53,660
A flammable lifting gas, used because the safe one was unavailable. A ship behind schedule. A hurried landing in charged air after a storm. A sharp turn that may have opened a leak.

34
00:06:54,960 --> 00:07:08,954
The airship industry never recovered. Its sister ship was finished, but never carried paying passengers. The older Graf Zeppelin was retired. Within a few years, the first commercial aeroplanes were flying the Atlantic.

35
00:07:10,254 --> 00:07:23,349
Statistically, airships had a reasonable safety record for their time. But one fire, caught on film, was enough to end them. Public confidence, once lost in thirty-four seconds, could not be rebuilt.

36
00:07:24,649 --> 00:07:39,386
The engineers had always known that hydrogen was dangerous. They had planned around it, with rules, procedures and care. For years, it worked. Then, on a stormy evening, behind schedule, all the conditions came together at once.

37
00:07:40,686 --> 00:07:45,400
Today, a marker on the field at Lakehurst shows where the Hindenburg came down.
